{"answer": "The critic's case fails on two independent grounds. First, the alleged #P-hardness of computing the intersection of all complete extensions is asserted without citation or reduction; it is not established. The intersection of all complete extensions is the skeptical acceptance set under complete semantics, which is decidable in polynomial time for a fixed framework by standard fixpoint iteration: the least complete labeling is computed in O(|A|·|R|) via the characteristic operator. There is no enumeration of extensions required, and the gate condition is a single fixpoint computation, not a counting problem. Second, the critic conflates general-case worst-case complexity with the actual structure of the Pass-2 gate. The gate evaluates a definability condition on a restricted subgraph (the set returned by Pass 1), not an arbitrary complete-extensions query over the full framework. No fact in the record establishes that this restricted check is #P-hard or super-polynomial. The polynomial cost guarantee is therefore not shown to be violated. The critic also offers a separate philosophical objection—that 'no new conflict' smuggles in a second semantic commitment violating SRC_001—but this is speculative; it is not a demonstrated inconsistency and concedes nothing about computational cost. Neither clause of the case is established: the complexity claim lacks any reduction or citation, and the semantic-conflict claim is unsupported conjecture."}